        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>Is the Pro 6 Arcadia Air Runner a real product, and where is it sold?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Yes. Pro 6 Fitness, part of the Golden Designs group in Ontario, California, sells it on its own store, where it was available at $4,595 on 14 October 2026. US dealers including Select Fitness, The Fitness Outlet and Sports Unlimited listed it at $4,495 to $4,595. We found no Amazon or Walmart listing.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What is the weight limit on the Pro 6 Arcadia Air Runner?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              No listing we found publishes one: not Pro 6’s own store and not any of the three dealers we checked. The 325 lb figure on the listing is the machine’s own weight, not a user limit. Ask the seller for the maximum user weight in writing before buying.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Does the Arcadia Air Runner need electricity?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              No. It is a non-motorized curved treadmill: the belt moves only when you push it, and you control speed by moving forward or back on the curve. Pro 6 says it needs no power. The listing does not say how the console is powered, so ask whether it takes batteries.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How many resistance levels does the Arcadia Air Runner have?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Six, according to Pro 6 and every dealer listing. Pro 6 says low settings give a near-frictionless glide for sprints and intervals and high settings simulate heavy sled pushes. The listing does not state the type of resistance.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>What warranty comes with the Pro 6 Arcadia Air Runner?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Pro 6 lists one year on parts for both residential and commercial use, with no frame or labour term stated. One dealer’s FAQ describes frame, parts and labour cover, which conflicts with its own specification table. Confirm the warranty in writing with your seller.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is the Arcadia Air Runner better than the AssaultRunner Pro?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              It offers something the AssaultRunner Pro does not — adjustable resistance — and a console that reads watts. The AssaultRunner Pro costs about $1,000 less, publishes a 350 lb user rating, carries a longer warranty and has independent coverage. For most home runners the AssaultRunner is the safer buy; the Arcadia makes sense if resistance is essential.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How much ceiling height does the Arcadia need?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Pro 6 lists a minimum ceiling height of 96 inches (8 feet). A curved deck raises you off the floor and running lifts you further, so measure to the lowest beam, duct or fitting above the running area, not to the highest point of the ceiling.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

        <!-- Why trust us -->
        <section id="why-trust-us" class="ed-trust not-prose">
          <h2>Why trust Treadmill Reviews USA?</h2>
          <p>
            Every recommendation is checked against the numbers that matter for a treadmill: the maker's own
            listing and manual, at least one independent source, the warranty terms and the CPSC recall
